1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,998
Every service on a server is a door. The firewall decides which doors are open.

2
00:00:06,018 --> 00:00:09,838
On Red Hat Enterprise Linux the firewall is firewalld,

3
00:00:09,858 --> 00:00:13,393
and you control it with one command: firewall-cmd.

4
00:00:13,863 --> 00:00:19,774
In this video: configure firewall settings using firewall-cmd and firewalld.

5
00:00:24,294 --> 00:00:26,877
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:27,347 --> 00:00:32,447
Configure firewall settings using firewall-cmd/firewalld.

7
00:00:32,917 --> 00:00:37,808
We'll look at zones, add a rule that is lost on reload, save a rule permanently,

8
00:00:37,828 --> 00:00:40,939
open a port in a zone, and change the default zone.

9
00:00:48,459 --> 00:00:51,156
Firewalld sorts traffic into zones.

10
00:00:51,176 --> 00:00:56,822
A zone is a set of rules, and each network interface belongs to one zone.

11
00:00:56,842 --> 00:00:58,918
The default zone is public.

12
00:00:58,938 --> 00:01:04,119
A rule allows a service, like http, or a port, like 8080 slash tcp.

13
00:01:14,589 --> 00:01:16,988
There are two copies of the rules.

14
00:01:17,008 --> 00:01:21,970
The runtime copy is active right now, and is lost on reload or reboot.

15
00:01:21,990 --> 00:01:24,531
The permanent copy is saved on disk.

16
00:01:24,551 --> 00:01:28,445
By default, firewall-cmd changes only the runtime copy.

17
00:01:28,465 --> 00:01:33,213
Before this demo, the lab silently installed and started firewalld,

18
00:01:33,233 --> 00:01:35,774
because this image does not have it.

19
00:01:51,294 --> 00:01:55,825
First, zones. get-default-zone shows the default zone:

20
00:01:55,845 --> 00:01:59,871
public. get-active-zones shows the zones in use:

21
00:01:59,891 --> 00:02:03,242
public, with the network interface ens3.

22
00:02:06,762 --> 00:02:09,580
list-all shows everything in a zone.

23
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:15,493
The allowed services are cockpit, dhcpv6-client and ssh. No ports are open.

24
00:02:15,513 --> 00:02:19,435
The other fields are empty, so they are not shown.

25
00:02:23,955 --> 00:02:31,121
Now a runtime rule. add-service=http opens the web service. Success means it worked.

26
00:02:31,141 --> 00:02:37,366
list-services shows http. Then reload, which reads the saved files again.

27
00:02:37,386 --> 00:02:41,130
And http is gone, because we never saved it.

28
00:02:45,650 --> 00:02:48,007
Again we add http at runtime.

29
00:02:48,027 --> 00:02:52,842
Now --runtime-to-permanent saves the running rules to disk.

30
00:02:52,862 --> 00:02:56,530
After a reload, http is still there. Careful:

31
00:02:56,550 --> 00:03:00,627
it replaces the saved rules with the running ones.

32
00:03:05,147 --> 00:03:11,702
Now a port in a named zone. --permanent saves the rule only on disk.

33
00:03:11,722 --> 00:03:18,953
--zone=public picks the zone. add-port=8080/tcp is port 8080, protocol TCP.

34
00:03:18,973 --> 00:03:24,754
A permanent rule is not active until a reload, so we reload,

35
00:03:24,774 --> 00:03:27,655
and list-ports shows 8080/tcp.

36
00:03:32,175 --> 00:03:38,450
Last, the default zone. --set-default-zone=work changes it, and it is saved at once.

37
00:03:38,470 --> 00:03:44,595
The default zone is now work, and the active zone is work, for the interface ens3.

38
00:03:44,615 --> 00:03:49,092
The zone public keeps its rules, but it is no longer in use.

39
00:03:49,112 --> 00:03:52,464
To undo, set the default zone back to public.

40
00:03:57,984 --> 00:04:01,420
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

41
00:04:02,940 --> 00:04:05,840
We reboot. The default zone is still work.

42
00:04:05,860 --> 00:04:10,984
And the zone public still has http and port 8080, because they were saved.

43
00:04:18,504 --> 00:04:22,899
Your turn. The lab silently installs firewalld first.

44
00:04:22,919 --> 00:04:29,230
In the zone public, add the port 9090/tcp and the service https, at runtime.

45
00:04:29,250 --> 00:04:34,812
Save them with --runtime-to-permanent. Set the default zone to dmz.

46
00:04:34,832 --> 00:04:38,644
Run the checker, then reboot and run it again.

47
00:04:49,114 --> 00:04:50,865
Try before you open the solution.

48
00:04:56,385 --> 00:04:59,053
Next up: manage default file permissions.

49
00:04:59,523 --> 00:05:02,426
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
